ng performance without  using encoders and we see a difference  of three times in favor of the mac pro  which is interesting because the raw  performance is over eight times faster  next i stabilized a one minute long 4k  hevc clip and the mac pro only took  eight seconds that is almost seven times  faster than real time which is crazy and  the short clips that we typically work  with will be finished in basically a  second so how long did the mac mini take  nine seconds  yes a whole second faster than the  system that costs 18 times more money  talk about efficiency the same thing  goes for exporting regular 4k footage  with multiple luts and even film grain  applied the mac pro takes 2 minutes and  28 seconds which is super fast but the  mini is not far behind just taking 36  seconds longer compared to the previous  mini that took over 12 minutes switching  to the more efficient hevc codec the  difference is even smaller in fact it is  now in favor of the m1 mini taking 2  minutes and 25 seconds compared to 2  minutes 30 seconds now i know what some  of you guys are gonna say this 6 000  graphics card is overkill for this type  of work while at the same time the mini  is perfectly optimized  while being relatively weak in specs and  you are absolutely right so i decided to  throw much harder workloads at these  systems but even then when it is slower  the difference isn't as large as you  would expect looking at the raw  performance and the prices the first was  four 4k files oversampled into a 4k  timeline each with film grain and two  luts applied with two clips being  reversed this is a test that i haven't  used in years because it was just too  difficult the mac pro took 4 minutes and  35 seconds to render this intense 5  minute project while the mac mini took 9  minutes and 15 seconds being severely  limited by its graphics so not eight  times faster but twice now i thought  that 4k prores raw files would also be  much faster with the mac pro and this  new graphics card but not really  exporting a graded five-minute project  took just two minutes and four seconds  but the mini wasn't far off at 2 minutes  and 55 seconds cpu and gpu being fully  utilized since the m1 is maxed out here  what happens if we throw graded 4k  progress raw at it and export with the  largest and highest quality codec well  it really slows down taking 35 minutes  for a five minute project but it is  still only three times slower when i  expected much more of a difference to  push this even further i thought of the  toughest thing in video editing  something that i excluded in the past  because it was just too painstakingly  slow and that is denoising 4k prores  rock lips surprisingly even with the  w6900x  you cannot get real-time playback so you  have to render and to render a 15 second  clip takes 9 minutes and 39 seconds yeah  that is very difficult to render but the  m1 mini took about 50 minutes to render  and i say about because i couldn't make  myself wait for it to finish but  basically it moved one percent every 30  seconds so what about outside of video  editing well the biggest reason to buy  one of these is for 3d rendering and man  is this thing much faster than the  previous best single graphics card the  vega 2. rendering in octane the standard  trench project takes just 22 seconds to  render compared to 51 seconds with the  vega 2 and 2 minutes and 24 seconds for  the m1 and for the chess scene just 12  seconds for the w6900x  and 1 minute and 42 seconds for the m1  mini yeah 12 seconds that is an insane  difference and if we do the math that  actually comes out to eight and a half  times faster performance which is  exactly what we see in the raw tear  flops so this is where the 6 000  graphics card really comes in for photo  and video editing the mac pro used to be  worth it but after optimizations and the  m1s unless you're doing insane stuff it  just is no longer worth it for those of  you that do 3d rendering every single  day that is where the brute force of  this 6 000 graphics card really comes in  and where it is worth spending the money  now i'm not sure if the software can be  optimized for m1 max any further just  like photo and video editing software  was but until that happens this is where  people are still going to have to buy  mac pros with high core counts and crazy  expensive graphics cards like these ones  now of course for our work even for  high-end video editing for example  10-bit footage from the r5 or the a7s2  the mac mini is actually faster at  rendering this out and not only that  it's way smoother at playing it back as  well because these high-end graphics  cards still don't have the proper  decoding chips built into them to handle  this footage so it is choppy and you  actually have to transcode the footage  before editing and when you're  transcoding it still takes way longer  about two and a half times longer to  transcode i thought that with this new  graphics card the mac pro might be worth  keeping if it was gonna be much faster  for a workload but as you guys see  because the encoders and decoders  haven't been changed and because  software being a limitation with these  codecs it really doesn't give you that  much extra performance and what it  really has me wondering is how much  faster are the new m1x max going to be  when they get rid of their main issue  which is raw graphics performance and  keep in mind the m1 graphics the a core  that's in here that is the slowest apple  graphics chip that they're ever going to  be making for the m1 and it's designed  for efficiency not maximum performance  so when apple starts targeting that how  insane are those ships going to be go  ahead let me know your thoughts down in  the comment section below go ahead and  click that circle above if you guys want  to subscribe to help us reach 1 million  subscribers which would be amazing check  out one of those great videos right over  there this has been max and i will